A patch of the Odin compiler (tag dev-2026-05, the `odin` of the Nixpkgs of
flake.nix). flake.nix applies it to the compiler of the development shell
and of the checks. The reason: property 11 (one source gives one module) and
choice NEW-1 of docs/FIRST_MILESTONE.md.

The fault. `-thread-count:1` does not give one thread. src/main.cpp,
init_global_thread_pool, makes `worker_count = thread_count` workers beside
the main thread ("// +1"). With `-thread-count:1` the pool has two threads,
and the worker steals the tasks that the main thread puts in its queue. Two
threads then run tasks of the checker at the same time:
check_collect_entities_all (src/checker.cpp) puts one task for each file,
and each task adds the entities of its file to the shared queue
`info.entity_queue` (mpsc_enqueue in add_entity_and_decl_info).
The order of that queue becomes the order of `info.entities`
(check_add_entities_from_queues), and the checker checks the global
entities in that order (check_all_global_entities, then
check_unchecked_bodies). The order of the checks decides the order of the
instances of polymorphic procedures and of the types in the LLVM module, and
the inlining of `-o:speed` follows that order. So rare builds of the same
source give other machine code. The entity id (`global_entity_id`) also
changes, and with it the name of each static variable of a procedure
(`.name-<id>`, src/llvm_backend_stmt.cpp, lb_build_static_variables).
Measured with the stock compiler, the process pinned to one processor:
`console` gave 3 distinct LLVM IR files in 60 builds, `host` 2 in 60.
`-no-threaded-checker` changes only check_procedure_bodies: the other
phases still use the pool.

The correction. `-thread-count:N` means N threads in all: the main thread and
N-1 workers, as the backend already counts (lb_generate_code,
lb_init_generator). With `-thread-count:1` no worker exists: the main
thread runs each task in thread_pool_wait, in one order. With no worker, a
thread that waits for a signal (wait_signal_until_available: the fields of
an #soa type that a queued task completes) runs the tasks of its own queue,
because no other thread runs them. With workers, that wait does not change.
Measured with this patch: `console` and `host` 60 builds each, one LLVM IR
file each, and one thread in each compiler process.

--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -14,10 +14,30 @@
 #include "build_settings.cpp"
 gb_global ThreadPool global_thread_pool;
 gb_internal void init_global_thread_pool(void) {
+	// -thread-count:N means N threads in all: the main thread and N-1 workers,
+	// as in the backend (lb_generate_code). With -thread-count:1 no worker
+	// exists, the main thread runs each task, and the order of the work is the
+	// same at each build: the same source gives the same output.
 	isize thread_count = gb_max(build_context.thread_count, 1);
-	isize worker_count = thread_count; // +1
+	isize worker_count = thread_count - 1;
 	thread_pool_init(&global_thread_pool, worker_count, "ThreadPoolWorker");
 }
+// run_one_queued_task_of_this_thread runs one task of the queue of the calling
+// thread, if the pool has no worker and the queue has a task. With no worker,
+// no other thread runs the tasks of a thread that waits for a signal
+// (wait_signal_until_available). With workers, nothing changes.
+gb_internal bool run_one_queued_task_of_this_thread(void) {
+	if (current_thread == nullptr || global_thread_pool.threads.count > 1) {
+		return false;
+	}
+	WorkerTask task;
+	if (thread_pool_queue_take(current_thread, &task) != Grab_Success) {
+		return false;
+	}
+	task.do_work(task.data);
+	global_thread_pool.tasks_left.fetch_sub(1, std::memory_order_release);
+	return true;
+}
 gb_internal bool thread_pool_add_task(WorkerTaskProc *proc, void *data) {
 	return thread_pool_add_task(&global_thread_pool, proc, data);
 }
--- a/src/threading.cpp
+++ b/src/threading.cpp
@@ -123,9 +123,15 @@
 	Futex futex;
 };
 
+gb_internal bool run_one_queued_task_of_this_thread(void);
+
 gb_internal void wait_signal_until_available(Wait_Signal *ws) {
-	if (ws->futex.load() == 0) {
-		futex_wait(&ws->futex, 0);
+	// With no worker, the task that sets the signal can be in the queue of
+	// this thread (an #soa type that completes later): run it, then sleep.
+	while (ws->futex.load() == 0) {
+		if (!run_one_queued_task_of_this_thread()) {
+			futex_wait(&ws->futex, 0);
+		}
 	}
 }
 
